║     SANSKRIT–ENGLISH LLM FINE-TUNING: ASSIGNMENT REPORT     ║
╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
1. PROBLEM UNDERSTANDING
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
The goal is to fine-tune a small open-source LLM to follow
instructions in the Sanskrit–English domain, covering:
  • Bidirectional translation (SA↔EN)
  • Verse/passage explanation
  • Grammar and morphology Q&A
  • Cultural/historical knowledge
  • Script and vocabulary tasks

Key challenges identified:
  1. Sanskrit is extremely low-resource in standard LLM training
     data (especially Devanagari script).
  2. Sanskrit's agglutinative morphology causes heavy subword
     fragmentation in BPE tokenizers.
  3. Ground-truth Sanskrit translations require expert knowledge
     to verify — BLEU/ROUGE metrics are imperfect proxies.
  4. Small GPU (T4, 16 GB) severely constrains model size and
     batch size.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2. DATASET PREPARATION
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
Three-tier dataset construction:

Tier-1 (Expert Curated, ~30 examples):
  • High-quality hand-crafted instruction–response pairs
  • Covers 8 task types: translation (both directions),
    verse explanation, grammar, morphology, Q&A, script,
    summarisation
  • Every example verified for accuracy

Tier-2 (Public HF Datasets, up to ~80 examples):
  • AI4Bharat Sangraha (Sanskrit filtered) – text completion
  • OPUS100 en-sa – parallel translation pairs
  • Graceful fallback if unavailable in the environment

Tier-3 (Synthetic Augmentation, ~120 examples):
  • Vocabulary Q&A (20 nouns × 2 directions)
  • Number translation (12 entries)
  • Verb morphology (10 verbs)
  • Templated paraphrase of instruction variants

Alpaca Format:
  ### Instruction: <task description>
  ### Input: <Sanskrit or English text> (optional)
  ### Response: <expected output>

This format is standard, model-agnostic, and allows
completion-only loss masking via DataCollatorForCompletionOnlyLM.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
3. BASE MODEL SELECTION
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
Primary: Llama-3.2-1B (Meta)
Fallback: TinyLlama-1.1B-Chat-v1.0

Reasons for Llama-3.2-1B:
  ✓ 1B params fits comfortably in T4 16 GB with 4-bit QLoRA
  ✓ 128k vocabulary BPE – better Devanagari coverage than
    earlier models
  ✓ Instruction-tuned variant available for chat fine-tuning
  ✓ Active community, well-tested with PEFT/TRL
  ✓ Permissive research licence

Alternatives considered:
  • Gemma-2B: Larger, tighter memory; good multilingual
    baseline but 2× the parameters.
  • IndicTrans2 (AI4Bharat): Purpose-built for Indic
    translation but encoder-decoder architecture is
    less flexible for instruction following.
  • mT5-base: Good multilingual coverage but weaker at
    open-ended instruction following.
  • Sarvam-1: Excellent for Indic languages but limited
    public availability at time of writing.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
4. FINE-TUNING APPROACH
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
Method: QLoRA (Quantised Low-Rank Adaptation)

QLoRA Setup:
  • 4-bit NF4 quantisation (BitsAndBytes) – base model frozen
  • LoRA rank r=16, alpha=32 – covers all 7 projection types
  • Trainable parameters ≈ 0.5% of total (≈5M of ~1B)
  • NF4 with double quantisation → ~6 GB GPU usage for 1B model

Why QLoRA over full fine-tuning:
  • Full fine-tuning 1B model in fp16 requires ~14 GB VRAM
    for weights alone + activations → doesn't fit on T4
  • QLoRA achieves comparable quality with <10% VRAM
  • Adapter weights are tiny (~50 MB) – easy to ship

Training configuration:
  • Optimizer: paged_adamw_8bit (memory-efficient AdamW)
  • LR: 2e-4, cosine schedule, 5% warmup
  • Batch: 4 × grad_accum 4 = effective batch 16
  • Epochs: 3
  • Loss: Cross-entropy on response tokens only
    (DataCollatorForCompletionOnlyLM masks instruction tokens)
  • Gradient checkpointing: enabled
  • FP16 mixed precision: enabled

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
5. HARDWARE CONSTRAINTS & OPTIMISATIONS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
Hardware: Google Colab T4 (16 GB VRAM, ~12 GB RAM)

Memory Budget (approximate):
  Base model (4-bit):    ~3.5 GB
  LoRA adapter:          ~0.3 GB
  Activations (batch=4): ~2.0 GB
  Optimiser states:      ~1.5 GB
  Tokenizer/buffers:     ~0.5 GB
  Total:                 ~7.8 GB  ← fits T4 comfortably

Key Optimisations Applied:
  1. 4-bit NF4 quantisation (BitsAndBytes)
  2. Double quantisation (saves ~0.4 bits/param more)
  3. paged_adamw_8bit (optimizer states in 8-bit)
  4. Gradient checkpointing (recompute activations)
  5. group_by_length=True (minimise padding)
  6. MAX_SEQ_LEN=512 (Sanskrit + English fit in this window)
  7. FP16 mixed precision
  8. dataloader_num_workers=0 (Colab stability)
  9. Completion-only loss (faster convergence, less noise)

