6. EVALUATION METHODOLOGY
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
Multi-level evaluation:

A. Held-out test set (5% of data)
   • BLEU (SacreBLEU) – standard MT metric
   • ROUGE-1, ROUGE-2, ROUGE-L – summary overlap
   • Per-task breakdown

B. Before/After comparison on 5 fixed prompts
   • Qualitative judgment of improvement
   • Covers all major task types

C. Extended qualitative eval on 5 novel prompts
   • Tests generalisation beyond training distribution

D. Error taxonomy analysis
   • 5-category error classification
   • Heuristic scoring on test predictions

Limitations of automatic metrics for Sanskrit:
  • BLEU/ROUGE are n-gram based; they penalise correct
    paraphrases and alternative valid translations
  • Devanagari character-level n-grams may not align
    even for semantically correct outputs
  • Human evaluation by a Sanskrit scholar would be
    the gold standard – acknowledged but out of scope here

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
7. FAILURE CASES
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
(See Cell 15 for detailed analysis)

  1. Transliteration script confusion (Roman vs Devanagari)
  2. Sandhi decomposition errors in compound words
  3. Vibhakti (case ending) misidentification
  4. Hallucinated verse attribution (wrong source text)
  5. Truncated generation for long explanations

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
8. CHALLENGES ENCOUNTERED
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
  1. Data scarcity: High-quality, verified Sanskrit instruction
     data is extremely limited. Most Sanskrit corpora are raw
     text (Wikipedia, Granthamala) without instruction pairs.

  2. Tokenizer mismatch: LLaMA's BPE tokenizer was not trained
     on Sanskrit; Devanagari gets fragmented into UTF-8 bytes,
     inflating sequence lengths by 2–3×.

  3. Evaluation difficulty: There is no established benchmark
     for Sanskrit instruction following. FLORES-200 has Sanskrit
     but only for translation, not instruction following.

  4. Model access: Llama-3.2 requires Hugging Face token and
     Meta licence approval; TinyLlama is the fallback.

  5. Colab instability: Long training sessions can disconnect;
     save_steps=100 mitigates data loss.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
9. WHAT I WOULD IMPROVE WITH MORE TIME
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
  1. TOKENIZER ADAPTATION:
     Train a custom SentencePiece model on Sanskrit+English
     corpus (AI4Bharat IndicCorp) and extend the base model's
     vocabulary with top-2000 Sanskrit unigrams. This would
     reduce sequence length by ~30% and improve morpheme
     boundary alignment.

  2. LARGER / BETTER BASE MODEL:
     Sarvam-1 or IndicTrans2 are purpose-built for Indic
     languages. On an A100 or L4 GPU, Gemma-2B QLoRA would
     also be feasible.

  3. LARGER DATASET:
     • OPUS Sanskrit–English parallel corpus (thousands of pairs)
     • Digitised Sanskrit literature from GRETIL
     • Synthetic data via GPT-4 with expert review
     • DCS (Digital Corpus of Sanskrit) – morphologically tagged

  4. RLHF / DPO:
     Use Direct Preference Optimisation with Sanskrit expert
     preferences to reduce hallucination in verse attribution.

  5. RETRIEVAL AUGMENTED GENERATION (RAG):
     Index a Sanskrit corpus (Vedabase, GRETIL) with a
     multilingual embedding model (LaBSE, mE5) to ground
     factual claims in retrieved passages.

  6. FORMAL EVALUATION BENCHMARK:
     Create a 200-example Sanskrit instruction eval set
     covering all 8 task types, scored by a human expert.

  7. CHUNKED SANDHI PREPROCESSING:
     Use a sandhi splitter (UoHyd Sanskrit tools) as
     preprocessing to normalise compound words before
     tokenisation.

